WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:06.673
A digital
first approach to the future of work

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:10.218
is where the industry
and all companies will be heading.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.386
Could the future of work

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:14.556
not be at home or in the office,
but in the metaverse instead?

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.808
There's that word.

00:00:16.808 --> 00:00:19.477
"Metaverse." "I'm doing well,
how are you doing?

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:22.522
When I think about the metaverse,
I think about immersive experiences

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.065
that have a sense of active presence.

00:00:24.065 --> 00:00:26.109
Wow. Look at this.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:27.277
This is me.

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:30.405
The metaverse is projected
to become over a trillion dollar market

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:32.115
by the end of this decade.

00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:35.910
And whether you know it or not, you'll
probably be working there in the future.

00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:38.913
We're moving into a world
where we're going to be interacting

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:40.415
with computers with our bodies.

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:42.625
This is definitely trippy.

00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:44.711
I think the adoption curve
is going to be slow.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:46.880
It's weird. I'm
a weird, floaty thing right now.

00:00:46.921 --> 00:00:47.922
Oh, look at you.

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:50.675
You have no legs. It's
kind of a fun world.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:53.094
And we're just at the very beginning
of a very long journey.

00:01:03.354 --> 00:01:06.608
Now, to properly tell this story,
I felt it was important that I,

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:10.361
A) never leave my home
and B) spend a bit more time

00:01:10.361 --> 00:01:12.906
in the metaverse myself

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:17.494
So I got a VR headset.

00:01:17.494 --> 00:01:18.787
Tried it on.

00:01:18.787 --> 00:01:20.163
Checked it out a little bit.

00:01:20.163 --> 00:01:23.124
I actually feel like this is what
I should look like all the time.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:26.086
And found
someone who's been immersed in AR / VR,

00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:29.714
commonly combined and referred to as XR,
since the beginning.

00:01:30.006 --> 00:01:31.591
"Oh, it says he's here." What up?

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:34.177
I caught you guys talking about me.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.138
My name is Kent Bye
and I do the voices of VR podcast.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.765
There's a sauna that's upstairs.

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:40.975
We did a scout for the right place
to do the interview.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.686
We considered a hot tub.

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:45.522
Now you're in. Feels great.

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:46.272
But ultimately,

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:49.567
we chose this quiet and comfortable
lounge, complete with the fireplace.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:54.072
We even created a virtual three camera
shoot with my colleagues, Tom and Jordan.

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:56.366
"I am rolling." It's the gang.

00:01:56.407 --> 00:01:57.992
We're getting the band back together.

00:01:57.992 --> 00:02:00.745
"I'm rolling on my end." "Cool.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:01.746
All right. Yeah. Take it away."

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.375
I mean,
it's just the whole idea of the avatar.

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:08.419
I find that interesting,
especially in the context of work

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:12.757
and meeting people that we choose
how we're going to be represented.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:15.844
I mean, I don't know
if I would be a ragdoll bunny rabbit

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:18.930
driving a robot in any other context.

00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:21.182
What's happening
is impacting your emotions.

00:02:21.182 --> 00:02:22.183
In some ways.

00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:25.061
You know, we're in these avatars,
and we have a sense of embodied presence.

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:25.937
But, you know,

00:02:25.937 --> 00:02:27.856
we can have the virtual body
ownership illusion

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:29.983
where you start to really own this body
that you have.

00:02:30.024 --> 00:02:31.651
When you have that full....

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:34.362
MATT: Kent's
obviously much better at this than me.

00:02:34.737 --> 00:02:37.407
You can tell how his hands move
really naturally when he talks.

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:41.119
Whereas mine, well,
they fidgeted and mostly just kind of

00:02:41.369 --> 00:02:42.704
folded in on themselves.

00:02:42.704 --> 00:02:45.915
Let me ask you, how did the pandemic
change our concept of meeting?

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:50.128
On the onset of the pandemic,
you had all this technology with Skype

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:53.631
and Zoom and everything that had been out
there as infrastructure for a long time.

00:02:53.631 --> 00:02:57.594
But most people would prefer to meet
and have that full, rich

00:02:57.594 --> 00:02:58.636
and bodied interaction.

00:02:58.636 --> 00:03:02.307
But after the pandemic,
pretty much everybody you could meet with.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.226
And so it actually kind of
democratized access to different people.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.064
And I think VR is kind of place
to kind of be in this next phase.

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:11.024
As we move forward.

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:11.441
You know,

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:13.109
we're having this conversation right now
where you didn't

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:16.279
have to come out to Portland, Oregon,
but we can still, you know,

00:03:16.321 --> 00:03:18.948
recreate the essence of what would happen
if you would be here.

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:22.160
This concept is being adopted
by a multitude of companies

00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:25.496
whose research has found that fully
simulated environments of immersive

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:28.208
learning helped their trainees
retain information better

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:32.670
as it gives them a stronger sense of place
and a more memorable experience overall.

00:03:33.171 --> 00:03:35.632
KENT:
Accenture is a huge consulting company.

00:03:35.632 --> 00:03:38.760
They hire anywhere from like 60,000
to 100,000 people a year.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:41.804
They've purchased
60,000 Quests to be able to send out

00:03:41.804 --> 00:03:44.599
to be able
to do onboarding into their company.

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:47.393
Whoa. Wait a second.

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:50.230
But what little time I've spent in VR
I couldn't help

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.483
but feel like it's more of an escape
rather than a place to work.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:55.109
And if I'm going to step

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:58.905
into an alternate reality,
I want to fly around, play games

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.032
and do things that aren't possible
in the real world,

00:04:01.282 --> 00:04:03.660
which doesn't
really include taking meetings.

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:08.289
KENT: I often
find within a lot of those workplaces

00:04:08.289 --> 00:04:11.542
they have the most amazing technology
in the world, and then you're in a world

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:15.463
that just makes you feel like you're
in a soulless corporate office.

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:19.175
And you go in there and you're like, "I
totally don't want to be in this space."

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:22.220
MATT: Nevertheless,
there is a mad dash in the tech

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.682
sector to create virtual spaces
that are conducive to work.

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:28.268
And some of the largest companies
are leading the charge.

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:32.313
As you probably already know., Facebook
rebranded to Meta,

00:04:32.397 --> 00:04:35.316
signaling their intention
to lead in this new virtual world.

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:38.319
So I got myself a slightly more buttoned

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:41.781
up, smoother and younger
looking avatar with much better hair.

00:04:41.864 --> 00:04:42.949
Whoa.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:44.617
That's kind of me.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:48.079
To check in with Meta's
Senior Product Director Micah Collins.

00:04:48.246 --> 00:04:50.957
I assume that you believe
that this is the future of work.

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:52.417
Is that correct?

00:04:52.417 --> 00:04:54.669
I believe it is
a part of the future of work.

00:04:54.711 --> 00:04:57.755
I think what companies and employees need

00:04:57.755 --> 00:05:01.134
is a new common collaboration platform.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.971
I remember how long we used to travel
for a 30 minute meeting

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:06.514
and take the redeye back home.

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:09.517
It's crazy to think about ever
having to do something like that again.

00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:13.479
We want to essentially allow a new culture

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:17.275
around collaboration to emerge
where people can kind of be

00:05:17.442 --> 00:05:21.404
however they're comfortable,
build a very inclusive product

00:05:21.404 --> 00:05:26.617
that still fosters options and the ability
to be as present as you want to be.

00:05:26.701 --> 00:05:29.412
"So, uh Barcelona, huh?"

00:05:30.621 --> 00:05:32.373
MATT: The gamification of reality

00:05:32.373 --> 00:05:36.294
has blurred those lines of what work
really is, how you get work done.

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:39.088
Why are we actually limiting ourselves

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:42.550
and constraining ourselves
to an office environment in the Metaverse?

00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:46.637
MICAH: Helping it
feel like a place for work is a key part.

00:05:46.637 --> 00:05:47.555
Is it familiar

00:05:47.555 --> 00:05:51.559
enough where we can feel like it's
something new, be inspired to be here,

00:05:51.559 --> 00:05:55.438
but not be completely distracted by it
so we don't actually get anything done?

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:59.400
I think it is that balance of
like building something special, but also

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.737
building it for a purpose with intention
to ultimately

00:06:02.737 --> 00:06:04.739
help us focus.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:09.035
MATT: Let's not forget
that meetings aren't always just talk.

00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:13.873
Fields like architecture, product design
or fabrication require hands

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:18.878
on collaborations, something that XR
is poised to take particular advantage of.

00:06:19.045 --> 00:06:22.673
MICAH: What does it look like
to sketch a new sneaker design together?

00:06:22.799 --> 00:06:26.928
What does it look like to review something
that's being designed?

00:06:26.969 --> 00:06:30.973
How do we think about things
like 3D object import from creation

00:06:30.973 --> 00:06:36.062
applications, tools that designers use
so that we can actually sit down and

00:06:36.687 --> 00:06:41.526
have people look at something together
and redline it in space together

00:06:41.526 --> 00:06:44.570
and then export
it for continued development.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.284
NEWS ANCHOR: Video conferences
can be mentally exhausting.

00:06:50.284 --> 00:06:54.539
"Many of us are also experiencing a
heightened level of anxiety and fatigue."

00:06:56.165 --> 00:06:57.959
MATT: But just as there's zoom fatigue,

00:06:57.959 --> 00:07:00.670
there's most certainly bound
to be XR fatigue.

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:03.923
In fact, I kind of already have it

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:06.968
and as intriguing, as all this is my head
kind of hurts.

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:08.261
My eyes are tired.

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:09.303
And in reality,

00:07:09.303 --> 00:07:12.807
not every meeting is necessarily
going to have to be this immersive.

00:07:13.182 --> 00:07:16.936
MICAH: There may be a headset on my desk
that's ready to go at any point in time.

00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:19.981
Let me start the meeting in VC
while I wrap up some texts

00:07:19.981 --> 00:07:21.190
and do some other things.

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:24.193
Then all of a sudden it's like, "Oh,
now it's time to put on my headset

00:07:24.735 --> 00:07:27.572
and go fully propelled
and immersive into the space."

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:32.660
We want to take that friction away
from moving

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:37.665
between different connection modalities
and just make it really up to the user.

00:07:38.916 --> 00:07:39.375
MATT: But as

00:07:39.375 --> 00:07:42.336
a new adopter, let me just say
we are not there yet.

00:07:44.505 --> 00:07:46.048
The utopia is that it's seamless.

00:07:46.048 --> 00:07:47.550
But let me throw a number at you.

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:52.555
Right now, the average
large company uses 188 different apps.

00:07:52.847 --> 00:07:56.058
188 I mean, that's kind of insane.

00:07:56.559 --> 00:07:59.729
We have tech overload right now.

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:02.440
We're still not even getting Zoom
calls right.

00:08:02.773 --> 00:08:05.359
"Didn't
you hear me, Judge?" "I can hear you.

00:08:05.359 --> 00:08:07.487
I think it's a filter." I'm here live.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.447
It's not that I'm not a cat.

00:08:09.447 --> 00:08:12.033
MATTHEW BOYLE: We're still screwing
those up left, right and center.

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:14.035
And you have people who feel excluded.

00:08:14.035 --> 00:08:16.829
You have workers
who are going into the office and spending

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:20.708
all day on Zoom and saying, "Why
did I schlep all the way into the office

00:08:20.875 --> 00:08:23.753
just to spend my day on Zoom?"
There's going to be cool experiments.

00:08:23.753 --> 00:08:25.129
Of course, there always are.

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:27.965
But, you know, in terms of this
being how we work,

00:08:27.965 --> 00:08:30.343
I think we're a little bit far from that
right now.

00:08:31.302 --> 00:08:32.845
The adoption curve is going to be slow.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:35.223
It's going to be painful.
It's going to be expensive.

00:08:35.473 --> 00:08:38.893
Companies are already spending
millions of dollars on new benefits

00:08:38.893 --> 00:08:41.854
to keep employees
happy and engaged so they don't quit.

00:08:42.104 --> 00:08:45.066
So now telling them what we got to be
investing in the metaverse as well,

00:08:45.066 --> 00:08:48.069
they're going to say, "Well, what's the
payoff?" "Why should we be doing this.?"

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:51.697
MATT: Back in the old fashioned
physical world,

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:55.576
a bit north of Silicon Valley.,
Microsoft has been actively trying

00:08:55.576 --> 00:08:59.956
to solve these more practical problems,
what they deem the hybrid workplace,

00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:04.001
a more equitable convergence of the remote
and office worlds.

00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:06.963
JARED:
When you think of meetings in the future,

00:09:07.004 --> 00:09:08.839
almost every interaction that you have in

00:09:08.839 --> 00:09:12.760
person is also going to have
some sort of digital person joining.

00:09:13.094 --> 00:09:15.930
They're going to be on the other side
digitally participating.

00:09:15.972 --> 00:09:16.472
And so you kind of

00:09:16.472 --> 00:09:20.142
have to design your physical space
for people who aren't physically there.

00:09:20.226 --> 00:09:23.145
It's our mission
to make sure that remote interactions

00:09:23.145 --> 00:09:26.649
are not second class interactions,
that they're first class,

00:09:26.983 --> 00:09:30.111
that any time we bring together
the digital with the physical

00:09:30.111 --> 00:09:34.532
that we really have
a very rich, multifaceted interaction

00:09:34.824 --> 00:09:37.660
that doesn't advantage or disadvantage
anyone.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:41.455
Because if we can tap into talent
from all over the world, if you don't have

00:09:41.455 --> 00:09:46.085
to physically be next to someone in order
to make a difference in an organization

00:09:46.335 --> 00:09:49.171
or in order
to make sure that your idea is used,

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:52.717
imagine what we can do for the planet
and for the human family, really.

00:09:53.676 --> 00:09:55.761
MATT:
This rethinking of the modern meeting

00:09:55.761 --> 00:09:59.932
includes a complete reconfiguration
of everything from technology interfaces

00:10:00.057 --> 00:10:03.936
and multiple camera angles to room sizes
and even table shapes.

00:10:04.687 --> 00:10:07.857
JARED: Ultimately, what we found
is that the most important thing is

00:10:07.857 --> 00:10:11.736
that you have to kind of balance people
seeing and interacting with each other

00:10:11.736 --> 00:10:15.656
physically and also being seen
by the digital participants.

00:10:15.948 --> 00:10:19.660
And so we have come to shapes
like you see here that allow cameras

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.539
to be able to create a separate video
feed for each of the physical participants

00:10:23.831 --> 00:10:27.001
that we can then beam over to people
who are attending virtually.

00:10:27.001 --> 00:10:29.045
That ends up being the sweet spot
right there.

00:10:31.464 --> 00:10:34.258
MATT: Of course, Microsoft
is still betting that XR will be a part

00:10:34.258 --> 00:10:35.676
of this future.

00:10:35.718 --> 00:10:38.679
Their version of Meta's
metaverse is called Mesh

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:40.598
They also have

00:10:40.598 --> 00:10:43.184
an ambitious mixed reality
headset, the HoloLens,

00:10:43.726 --> 00:10:46.979
the adoption of which has yet to be wide
enough to project any dominance

00:10:46.979 --> 00:10:50.775
in the market, at least partially due
to its very high price tag.

00:10:51.275 --> 00:10:53.486
And that very well may be
where Apple comes in.

00:10:53.736 --> 00:10:56.739
MARK: Apple is the elephant in the room
when it comes to virtual meetings,

00:10:56.739 --> 00:11:00.701
virtual reality and augmented reality
The company for about seven years

00:11:00.701 --> 00:11:04.747
now has been working on a VR headset
or a mixed reality headset, augmented

00:11:04.747 --> 00:11:06.916
reality headset.
They have people from NASA.

00:11:07.124 --> 00:11:08.709
They have people from the video game
industry.

00:11:08.709 --> 00:11:10.086
They have people from Hollywood.

00:11:10.086 --> 00:11:12.880
They have people who worked
on the original, you know, Macs, iPhones,

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.092
iPads, Apple watches,
leading the design of this project

00:11:16.092 --> 00:11:19.512
from a software standpoint, a hardware
standpoint, a content standpoint.

00:11:20.888 --> 00:11:21.472
MATT: With Apple's

00:11:21.472 --> 00:11:24.600
reputation for relatively affordable
consumer products.

00:11:24.809 --> 00:11:25.601
There's little doubt

00:11:25.601 --> 00:11:28.729
that whatever they drop
will alter the landscape of the metaverse

00:11:29.397 --> 00:11:32.608
But while the older kids fight
among themselves in the great tech

00:11:32.608 --> 00:11:37.571
sandbox in the sky, we mustn't forget that
the metaverse remains anyone's game.

00:11:38.114 --> 00:11:41.575
There's a diverse set of visions out there
that could easily have an impact

00:11:41.575 --> 00:11:43.077
on the workplace of the future.

00:11:43.077 --> 00:11:47.498
And many of them don't involve AR, VR
or any kind of mixed reality at all.

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:51.919
Desktop versions of meeting places
can achieve similar results

00:11:52.128 --> 00:11:53.671
without the adoption issues

00:11:53.671 --> 00:11:56.924
or infrastructure
requirements of VR headsets and the like.

00:11:57.466 --> 00:12:00.511
ALEX: What we find is companies
that are maybe VR first.

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:03.055
There's that novel kind of cool factor.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.433
But not many people have headsets yet.

00:12:05.558 --> 00:12:07.977
That's why we're desktop first
application.

00:12:08.436 --> 00:12:11.313
MATT: Virbela is betting that
physical offices of any kind

00:12:11.355 --> 00:12:12.773
are a thing of the past.

00:12:12.773 --> 00:12:15.860
Offering an entire virtual campus,
complete with everything

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:18.529
from private offices to convention halls,

00:12:18.946 --> 00:12:22.700
auditoriums, music
venues, yoga studios, soccer fields.

00:12:22.992 --> 00:12:26.620
We even took a boat
ride "Why don't you drive"?" "Oh, wow."

00:12:29.081 --> 00:12:31.375
ALEX:
We see environments like this becoming

00:12:31.375 --> 00:12:34.754
kind of the hub where large organizations
can come together

00:12:35.004 --> 00:12:38.424
and have this united place of
this is our headquarters.

00:12:39.258 --> 00:12:43.220
When the pandemic hit,
we were about 20 employees, and today

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.765
we're closer to 140.

00:12:47.016 --> 00:12:50.728
MATT: Everyone here is hundreds, if
not thousands of miles from one another.

00:12:50.895 --> 00:12:53.814
Yet present together
in this virtual campus.

00:12:54.398 --> 00:12:58.986
ALEX: Nice, casual place to meet
space gives us again these social cues,

00:12:59.195 --> 00:13:03.240
the ability to sit, even though obviously
our avatars aren't getting tired.

00:13:03.699 --> 00:13:06.911
We see people getting territorial
about their space.

00:13:07.536 --> 00:13:09.038
MATT: At one point,
we crashed a conference

00:13:09.038 --> 00:13:10.664
and open the floor up to comments.

00:13:10.664 --> 00:13:12.666
I asked people to tell me
where they were physically,

00:13:12.917 --> 00:13:15.586
and they were indeed
thousands of miles away from each other.

00:13:15.795 --> 00:13:17.713
ALEX:
Wait, who's working from Disney World?

00:13:20.716 --> 00:13:22.384
MATT: That is completely insane.

00:13:22.384 --> 00:13:23.302
Well, thanks, everybody.

00:13:23.302 --> 00:13:26.138
Thanks for letting us interrupt
your meeting here.

00:13:26.138 --> 00:13:28.140
Just the idea that someone could clock

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:31.727
in work hours while at the Magic Kingdom
kind of blew my mind.

00:13:32.228 --> 00:13:33.854
Here was someone able to balance work

00:13:33.854 --> 00:13:36.982
and pleasure in a way that was unthinkable
a few years prior.

00:13:38.108 --> 00:13:40.986
But after about 2 hours
inside this campus, I couldn't help

00:13:40.986 --> 00:13:44.740
but recall what Kent told me
about the state of virtual workplaces.

00:13:44.782 --> 00:13:45.491
KENT: You're in a world

00:13:45.491 --> 00:13:49.370
that just makes you feel like you're
in a soulless corporate office.

00:13:49.453 --> 00:13:52.748
You go in there and you're like, "I
totally don't want to be in this space."

00:13:53.707 --> 00:13:55.835
MATT: Whereas I do
get the idea that work needs

00:13:55.835 --> 00:13:59.046
to stay grounded
in some sort of analog of reality.

00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:02.174
There's a part of me
that just wants to abandon all constraints

00:14:02.174 --> 00:14:03.759
of the physical world.

00:14:03.759 --> 00:14:07.847
And that thinking led me quite literally
"Nowhere." What's up, buddy?

00:14:07.972 --> 00:14:09.723
Welcome to Nowhere.

00:14:09.723 --> 00:14:13.686
JON: This is a virtual 3D metaverse
platform.

00:14:14.228 --> 00:14:16.814
But Nowhere, we're not about recreating
reality, like

00:14:16.814 --> 00:14:19.859
by putting buildings and office
rooms and board spaces.

00:14:20.192 --> 00:14:23.571
We're about creating environments
that really inspire different emotions

00:14:23.571 --> 00:14:25.030
and different feelings about

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:27.658
how do you want to feel
when you're in these enviroments?

00:14:27.658 --> 00:14:30.995
Actually, why don't I take you to where
I love to have my meetings?

00:14:31.912 --> 00:14:33.455
Nice. There we go.

00:14:35.541 --> 00:14:35.791
MATT:

00:14:35.791 --> 00:14:38.586
Nowhere embraces magical realism
as its backdrop.

00:14:38.836 --> 00:14:41.589
It's decidedly avatar free web based,

00:14:41.714 --> 00:14:44.425
and for lack of a better word,
kind of anti-VR.

00:14:45.301 --> 00:14:48.304
Instead, they've constructed a platform
that takes the best of both video

00:14:48.304 --> 00:14:50.514
conferencing and virtual spaces

00:14:51.932 --> 00:14:54.560
JON: For us, there's
nothing like the presence of....

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:55.686
of humans.

00:14:55.686 --> 00:14:57.813
When you're on a zoom call. It's
just a flat grid.

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:00.357
You know, you see the people's faces,
but there's no sense of presence.

00:15:00.608 --> 00:15:01.525
But within Nowhere,

00:15:01.525 --> 00:15:05.070
you have that added sense of place
that it allows you to create memories.

00:15:05.362 --> 00:15:06.488
You know, we're looking at each other.

00:15:06.488 --> 00:15:08.657
We're able to see all the subtlety
of facial expression,

00:15:08.908 --> 00:15:12.411
which is based on thousands
and thousands of years of evolution of us

00:15:12.411 --> 00:15:17.333
instinctually, you know, looking for signs
of how we read people's faces

00:15:17.333 --> 00:15:19.501
and how we read people's body behavior.

00:15:19.501 --> 00:15:21.879
When I'm in VR,
I feel disconnect from people

00:15:21.879 --> 00:15:23.672
and I feel disconnect from the world.

00:15:23.672 --> 00:15:27.801
You know, some of my most exciting moments
in life are when I'm with humans.

00:15:28.344 --> 00:15:32.640
I don't believe that cartoon avatars are
going to create the feeling of co presence

00:15:32.932 --> 00:15:36.435
and the feeling of intimacy
that we need within work environments

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:40.022
to trust each other,
to collaborate freely, and to really build

00:15:40.022 --> 00:15:43.025
relationships that build great
teams, that build great products.

00:15:45.235 --> 00:15:47.863
MATT: Regardless of Jon's hard line
stance on avatars.

00:15:48.155 --> 00:15:50.991
It seems obvious that XR is not going away

00:15:51.367 --> 00:15:54.703
The AR / VR and mixed reality sectors
combined

00:15:54.787 --> 00:15:58.958
is expected to grow
into a $100 billion market by 2025.

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:00.501
And with the availability

00:16:00.501 --> 00:16:04.338
of more affordable headsets, adoption
is really a matter of when not if.

00:16:04.713 --> 00:16:06.215
MARK: The next paradigm is really

00:16:06.215 --> 00:16:09.593
these augmented reality, mixed reality
and virtual reality ecosystems.

00:16:09.885 --> 00:16:13.097
And right now it seems
that Meta is going to be at the forefront

00:16:13.847 --> 00:16:17.142
so you're going to see a wide array of
people probably using those Meta headsets

00:16:17.434 --> 00:16:18.560
for video conferencing.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:20.562
More people at home
will be able to get their hands on it.

00:16:20.562 --> 00:16:25.067
And at some point, Apple's become a big
deal too, for the future of virtual work.

00:16:26.026 --> 00:16:27.695
MATT: I'm
still wrapping my head around all of this.

00:16:29.279 --> 00:16:30.364
VR and the like,

00:16:30.364 --> 00:16:34.660
fully replacing our physical reality
isn't likely to happen anytime soon.

00:16:35.619 --> 00:16:39.581
But as alternative realities of every type
seep into our homes and offices,

00:16:39.581 --> 00:16:41.291
be them virtual or otherwise,

00:16:41.291 --> 00:16:44.962
it's safe to say that this will not be
our last meeting in the Metaverse.

00:16:45.879 --> 00:16:47.715
Guys, this has been really fun.
I think we should.

00:16:47.715 --> 00:16:48.590
I think we should do this again.

